---
import PageNav from "./PageNav.astro";
import StackSample from "./StackSample.astro";
import { chartPage } from "../data/docs-catalog";
import { playgroundHref, smallestDonutSpec, specText } from "../data/donut-teach";

const chart = chartPage("donut");
const href = "/docs/charts/donut";
const spec = specText(smallestDonutSpec);
const play = playgroundHref(smallestDonutSpec);
const hole = `{
  "mark": { "innerRadius": 0.6 }
}`;
const gap = `{
  "mark": { "padAngle": 2 }
}`;
const labels = `{
  "mark": { "labels": true, "labelPosition": "outside" }
}`;
const legend = `{
  "legend": { "position": "bottom" }
}`;
const palette = `{
  "mark": {
    "palette": ["#0f6e56", "#c45c26", "#1d4e89", "#5b4b8a"]
  }
}`;
const sort = `{
  "mark": { "sort": "desc" }
}`;
const start = `{
  "mark": { "startAngle": 90 }
}`;
const titled = `{
  "encoding": {
    "x": "label",
    "y": { "field": "value", "title": "Sessions" }
  }
}`;
---

<h1>Donut</h1>
<p>
  A donut shows how a few categories add up to one total. Each category is a slice, and the hole in the middle leaves room for that total.
</p>

<section id="when-to-use">
  <h2>When to use it</h2>
  <p>
    Use a donut for parts of a whole, with a handful of categories. Two to six slices stay readable. The centre total gives the reader the sum without a second chart.
  </p>
  <p>
    A line is the chart for a time series. A bar is the chart for comparing totals, and for a long list of categories. Many thin slices turn the ring into a legend. The eight-source example on this page is there so you can see that limit.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="pie">
  <h2>Donut and pie</h2>
  <p>
    Pie and donut share one arc renderer. The <code>type</code> selects the chart and its defaults.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>type: "donut"</code> keeps the hole. <code>mark.innerRadius</code> defaults to <code>0.6</code>, and <code>mark.padAngle</code> defaults to <code>2</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>type: "pie"</code> is the solid disk. <code>mark.innerRadius</code> defaults to <code>0</code>, and <code>mark.padAngle</code> defaults to <code>0</code>.
  </p>
  <p>A closed hole is a pie. Set <code>type: "pie"</code>.</p>
</section>

<section id="data">
  <h2>Data</h2>
  <p>
    Each row is one category and one number. Point <code>encoding.x</code> at the category and <code>encoding.y</code> at the number. The channel is <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>. It is not <code>theta</code> or <code>angle</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The numbers are amounts. ChartKit adds the slices it draws and gives each slice its share of that sum. You do not pass percentages. The spec below happens to add to 100. The yard example uses 86, 54, 41, and 28, and the fleet example uses 1840 and 1260.
  </p>
  <p>
    A missing number, a zero, or a negative number is left out of the ring. <code>validate()</code> still accepts those rows. If every row is left out, <code>render()</code> returns an error chart, CK1006, instead of a ring.
  </p>
  <p>
    When the hole is wide enough, the centre prints the sum of the drawn slices. A field name of <code>value</code> or <code>y</code> stays unlabeled. Any other field name is the unit under the total. A channel <code>title</code> replaces that name.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>encoding.y.title</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{titled}</code></pre>
  </figure>
  <p><code>a11y.alt</code> is required. One sentence, 10 to 500 characters, with the point of the chart.</p>
</section>

<section id="spec">
  <h2>A spec that renders</h2>
  <p>
    This is the smallest donut. <code>version</code>, <code>type</code>, and <code>a11y.alt</code> are required. The hole and the gap come from the donut defaults, so <code>mark</code> can be omitted.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>spec.json</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{spec}</code></pre>
  </figure>
  <p>Install core and the adapter for your framework. The menu in the sidebar picks the snippet. <code>spec</code> is the JSON above.</p>
  <div class="facts" aria-label="Donut packages">
    {
      chart.facts.map((fact) => (
        <span class="fact">
          <span class="fact__k">{fact.k}</span>
          <span class="fact__v">{fact.v}</span>
        </span>
      ))
    }
    <span class="fact">
      <span class="fact__k">Family</span>
      <span class="fact__v">{chart.family}</span>
    </span>
  </div>
  <StackSample />
</section>

<p>Each option below is a key on the spec. The demo changes that key and updates the chart and the snippet together.</p>

<section class="concept" id="hole">
  <h2>Hole</h2>
  <p>
    <code>mark.innerRadius</code> is the hole as a fraction of the radius, from 0 to 0.95. A donut defaults to 0.6. The centre total is drawn when the hole is wide enough to hold the number.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>mark.innerRadius</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{hole}</code></pre>
  </figure>
</section>

<section class="concept" id="gap">
  <h2>Gap</h2>
  <p>
    <code>mark.padAngle</code> is the gap between slices, in degrees, from 0 to 10. A donut defaults to 2. The renderer spends that many degrees on the gap and shares the rest of the circle by value.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>mark.padAngle</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{gap}</code></pre>
  </figure>
</section>

<section class="concept" id="labels">
  <h2>Slice labels and the legend</h2>
  <p>
    <code>mark.labels</code> draws a value on each slice. It defaults to false. <code>mark.labelPosition</code> sets the place. <code>inside</code> and <code>center</code> sit on the slice. <code>outside</code> draws a leader line. Setting <code>labelPosition</code> turns the labels on. With no <code>mark.labelFormat</code>, the label is the share of the total. Overlapping labels are skipped.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>mark.labels</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{labels}</code></pre>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <code>legend</code> is the key beside the ring. Omit it, or set <code>true</code>, and a donut with more than one slice shows a legend. <code>false</code> hides it. <code>legend.position</code> is <code>top</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, <code>left</code>, or <code>inside</code>. When position is omitted, a chart at least twice as wide as it is tall puts the legend on the right. Otherwise the legend sits along the bottom. <code>legend.values</code> prints each slice's number next to its name.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>legend.position</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{legend}</code></pre>
  </figure>
</section>

<section class="concept" id="colours">
  <h2>Colours</h2>
  <p>
    Omit <code>mark.palette</code> and each slice takes the next theme colour. Set <code>mark.palette</code> to a list of colours, in slice order, to override that list. One to 24 colours. <code>encoding.color</code> can name a field on the row. It is the same channel other charts use.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>mark.palette</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{palette}</code></pre>
  </figure>
</section>

<section class="concept" id="sort">
  <h2>Sort</h2>
  <p>
    <code>mark.sort</code> orders the slices. <code>none</code> keeps the data order and is the default. <code>asc</code> and <code>desc</code> sort by the number.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>mark.sort</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{sort}</code></pre>
  </figure>
</section>

<section class="concept" id="start">
  <h2>Start angle</h2>
  <p>
    <code>mark.startAngle</code> is where the first slice starts, in degrees. <code>0</code> is the top, and the slices run clockwise. The value can be from -360 to 360.
  </p>
  <figure class="code">
    <figcaption><span>mark.startAngle</span></figcaption>
    <pre><code>{start}</code></pre>
  </figure>
</section>

<section id="more">
  <h2>The rest of the spec</h2>
  <p>
    <code>mark.cornerRadius</code> rounds the slice corners, in px, from 0 to 40. <code>mark.other</code> folds slices under a fraction of the total into one bucket. <code>mark.explode</code> names slices to pull out, and <code>mark.offset</code> is how far, in px. Theme, number format, and the other shared keys live on the playground, with the full option list.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="related">
  <h2>Related charts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/docs/charts/pie">Pie</a> is the solid disk. The spec type is <code>pie</code>.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/charts/rose">Rose</a> gives every category the same angle and uses the radius for the value.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/charts/radial-bar">Radial bar</a> puts categories around a circle and uses the length of the bar for the value.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a href={play}>Open this spec in the playground</a>. That link opens the spec on this page. The demo link opens the chart you are looking at, including any option you change.
  </p>
</section>

<PageNav href={href} />
